// Rejected at resolve (typing): a scan's order key must be tie-free, and here
// nothing says it is (ADR 0029 Decision 11's tier 1; the theorem is
// `Mensura.IsArrangement.unique`).
//
// `taken_at` is an ordinary attribute of a bag store, so several readings of one
// machine may share it.  With ties there is no canonical arrangement, so the
// scan's intermediate values depend on how the rows happened to be stored: same
// input, different output across runs, which is exactly the class of bug the
// type system exists to prevent.
//
// This is the ordered counterpart of `reduce_bag_without_completeness.mensura`.
// Both are properties of the *data*, undecidable in general, and both are
// established upstream or admitted by fiat.  Two ways to fix this file:
//
//   - make the time part of the identity and `demote` it, so the fact is
//     *derived* (`Mensura.keyInjOn_demote_tag`); see
//     `accept/qualified_series_windows.mensura`;
//   - claim it with `assume { arranged }`; see
//     `accept/scan_with_a_descending_key.mensura`.
import series

unit Machine {
  id: string
}

store readings {
  unit { Machine }
  attr* {
    temperature: real
    taken_at: date
  }
}

view bad {
  readings
    |> map_bags |k, b| (
      .peak = series.running_max (|r| r.temperature) (|r| r.taken_at) b
    )
}
